== Exercise 4.6: deep Huffman trees
OK   4.6(a) the arrays: (1,1,1,3,4,7,11,18) and (1,1,1,2,3,5,8,13) for n = 8
OK   4.6(a) Lucas array: EVERY Huffman tree has depth n-1 (n = 1..18)
OK   4.6(a) Fibonacci array F_1..F_n: every Huffman tree has depth n-1 (n = 1..18)
OK   4.6(a) (1,1,1,2,3,5,...): SOME Huffman tree has depth n-1 (n = 1..18)
OK   4.6(a) ... but not every one for n >= 4 (ties)
OK   4.6(a) exhaustive (n <= 8): smallest possible largest frequency, every tree deep: 1,1,1,3,4,7,11,18 = L_{n-2} for n >= 3  [{1: (1,), 2: (1, 1), 3: (1, 1, 1), 4: (1, 1, 1, 3), 5: (1, 1, 1, 3, 4), 6: (1, 1, 1, 3, 4, 7), 7: (1, 1, 1, 3, 4, 7, 11), 8: (1, 1, 1, 3, 4, 7, 11, 18)}]
OK   4.6(a) exhaustive (n <= 8): smallest largest frequency if ties may go your way: 1,1,1,2,3,5,8,13 = F_{n-1} for n >= 2
OK   4.6(a) L_{n-2} < F_n for n >= 5, L_{n-2} = F_n for n = 4 (Fibonacci is not optimal)
OK   4.6(a) Fibonacci: F_1 + ... + F_k = F_{k+2} - 1; L_m = F_{m-1} + F_{m+1}; n = 5: L_3 = 4 vs F_5 = 5
OK   4.6(a) (1,1,1,2): Huffman trees of depth 3 and of depth 2 both occur
OK   4.6(a) n = 6 figure: (1,1,1,3,4,7), internal weights 2, 3, 6, 10, 17, every tree has depth 5
OK   4.6(b) Fibonacci total N = F_{n+2} - 1
OK   4.6(a) identity S_k = f_1 + ... + f_k = L_k - 1 for the Lucas array (k >= 2)
OK   4.6(b) in random Huffman trees every grandparent weighs at least twice its grandchild
OK   4.6(b) every leaf depth is at most 2 log2 N + 1
OK   4.6(b) the bound is about right for Fibonacci frequencies: depth n-1 vs 2 log2 N + 1
== Exercise 4.8: optimal ternary codes
OK   4.8 ternary algorithm cost = Kraft brute force optimum (random ties), n = 1..7  [790 inputs]
OK   4.8 n = 4, f = (1,1,1,1): algorithm cost 6 (lengths 1,1,2,2), naive cost 7 (lengths 1,2,2,2)
OK   4.8 for odd n the naive version coincides with the algorithm (no dummy needed)
OK   4.8 n = 1: cost 0 (empty codeword); n = 2: both codewords have length 1
OK   4.8 reduction: optimal code cost = optimal FULL ternary tree cost after adding one 0 when n is even
